The routine exp-s computes e to the power x, for x below ln 10, by pseudo-multiplication.

Input: v from 0 to 2302, standing for x = v / 1000, below ln 10, in two parts: its last three digits and its
thousands. Result: 100 e^x rounded, from 100 to 999, the three significant digits of e^x, which is from 1 to 10.
For a larger x the caller subtracts 2303 from v as often as needed and counts one more power of ten each time;
each such step costs 0.0004 of relative error, since ln 10 is 2.302585.

Call: put the thousands of v in cell 53, load its last three digits into the accumulator and JMP 12. It returns
with 100 e^x in the accumulator.
Cells 12-98 (87 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 33, 41, 52, 62-67, 98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 911 instructions a call, 525.8 on average over the domain, counted from the calling JMP to the
return jump. Worst error: 1 in the last digit (units of the third significant digit) against the correctly rounded
value, at v = 1; 522 of the 2,303 inputs are off.
Address bases: cell 54 holds SUB 57. To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from every address in its
instructions that points into cells 12-98 and from the address bases; addresses 00 and 99 stay as they are.

